IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Row il-44. THE NOUNS OF A CROSS-REFERENCE, for section 12A1 of the Entry into Israel Law, 5712-1952.
-- This row is not an encoding of the Law. It records the cross-references of one section as typed values the tests check, and keeps the one defect it found as an expected-red test.
-- No rule, no sentence, no deadline: only the kinds of thing a reference record talks about.
--
-- THE KINDS BELOW ARE MINE, not the Law's: each is a short plain description I wrote from reading the citing words and the target's heading or text, so that two descriptions of the same thing are the same kind.
-- The quoted lines beside each reference in `il44-references.l4` are the Law's words (src: lines, expanded mechanically from the deposit) or the other instruments' words (ext: lines).
--
-- THE LAW'S TWO SHORT NAMES. Section 12A1(a) of the Law defines both of them in its first words: "the Criminal Procedure Law" is the Criminal Procedure Law [Consolidated Version], 5742-1982,
-- and "the Criminal Procedure Ordinance" is the Criminal Procedure Ordinance (Arrest and Search) [New Version], 5729-1969. Every citing text in this row uses one of the two short names.

§ `Nouns of a cross-reference`

-- The two instruments the short names stand for.
DECLARE `A Criminal Procedure instrument` IS ONE OF
    `the Criminal Procedure Law [Consolidated Version], 5742-1982`
    `the Criminal Procedure Ordinance (Arrest and Search) [New Version], 5729-1969`

-- What a section is about. For a section that no longer has text, the only honest description is that it has none.
DECLARE `What a section is about` IS ONE OF
    `the power of a police officer to seize an article`
    `the court's order handing a seized article to a claimant`
    `the court's forfeiture order after a conviction`
    `trial in the accused's absence, where the accused was summoned to the start of the trial`
    `trial in the absence of an accused who did not appear later in the trial`
    `no text: the section is repealed`

-- How a text was read. Two statuses and nothing else; the status is a typed value, so a test can see it.
DECLARE `How a text was read` IS ONE OF
    `read from the page image of the official instrument`
    `read in the Wikisource consolidation only`

-- A cross-reference is TWO descriptions of one thing: what the citing words say the cited section is about, and what that section of that instrument is about.
DECLARE `A cross-reference` HAS
    `the citing words are in` IS A STRING
    `the citing words use the short name of` IS A `A Criminal Procedure instrument`
    `the section number the citing words give` IS A NUMBER
    `the citing words say that section is about` IS A `What a section is about`
    `that section of that instrument is about` IS A `What a section is about`
    `the citing words were read` IS A `How a text was read`
    `the target was read` IS A `How a text was read`

§ `Nouns of a citation by address`

-- The second kind of record: a citation that gives a section or paragraph by its NUMBER ("section 13(d)", "section 12(1)"), where the number now names something else, or nothing.
-- Again the kinds are mine, as above: each is the topic the citing words give, or the topic of the provision at the address, in the few words the row needs.
DECLARE `What a numbered provision is about` IS ONE OF
    `the order of removal given to a person`
    `the duty to remove a person found in Israel without a residence licence`
    `the offence in paragraph (1) of section 12`
    `no provision at that address`

-- What this row knows of instruments after the one that wrote the citing words.
DECLARE `What is known of later instruments` IS ONE OF
    `no later instrument was read`
    `the consolidation lists a later instrument, which was not read`
    `the consolidation lists no later instrument, and none was read`

DECLARE `A citation by address` HAS
    `the citing words are in` IS A STRING
    `the citing words give the address` IS A STRING
    `the citing words say that provision is about` IS A `What a numbered provision is about`
    `the provision at that address in the text as it stands is about` IS A `What a numbered provision is about`
    `the matter the citing words mean now stands at` IS A STRING
    `the citing words were read` IS A `How a text was read`
    `the target was read` IS A `How a text was read`
    `later instruments that touch the citing words` IS A `What is known of later instruments`

-- A waiting period given in two texts. A day is read as 24 hours (fork P1 in NOTES.md).
DECLARE `A period in a text` HAS
    `the text is` IS A STRING
    `the period it gives, in hours` IS A NUMBER
    `the period was read` IS A `How a text was read`
